BUILD_ONLY: ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/eai-autobio:<images/autobio/VERSION> is not pushed to a registry (docs/PROTOCOL.md §2.4,
D5). Every evaluation host builds it locally; the AutoBio tasks reference that tag and Harbor finds it in the local
Docker image store.

Why
  AutoBio's repository (github.com/autobio-bench/AutoBio, one commit, 0850421, 2025-05-28, "Preliminary version of
  AutoBio") has no LICENSE file and no licence statement for its code or for its own assets: the digitized instruments,
  tubes, racks and pipette, and two binaries without source (libmjlab.so.3.3.0, the thread and detent MuJoCo plugins,
  and meshplane.so, the liquid-surface solver). The robot models it bundles keep their own licences (UR5e and Robotiq
  2F-85: BSD, ROS-Industrial; ALOHA 2: BSD, Trossen Robotics; DexHand021: Apache-2.0; the display font: SIL OFL). The
  "CC BY-NC-SA 4.0" on the paper's arXiv page is the paper's own licence; the recorded demonstrations on Hugging Face
  (huggingface.co/autobio-bench) are tagged MIT, which covers those datasets, not the simulator. Without a licence
  nothing gives us the right to redistribute the code or the assets, so:
  - the image is built on each host from AutoBio's own public repository and never published;
  - this repository contains none of AutoBio's files: the tasks' tooling imports AutoBio from the image, the frozen
    instances are seeds and state hashes, and the reference solutions are control sequences recorded by running
    AutoBio's experts (data produced by the program, like the authors' MIT-tagged datasets).
  A licence from the authors that allows redistribution would let a maintainer delete this file; nothing else changes.

What the builder accepts
  Using AutoBio's unlicensed code and assets for research evaluation on their own machine, the licences of the robot
  models and the font bundled with it (assets/robot/*/LICENSE, assets/common/font/LICENSE in the checkout), MuJoCo's
  Apache-2.0 licence, and the licences of the pinned pip packages (images/autobio/requirements.txt). No account, key
  or token is needed.

Data
  None beyond the image: the assets come with the clone (75 MB); nothing is mounted at run time. The frozen task
  instances live in the task directories.

Host
  Linux x86_64 and Docker. No GPU: MuJoCo physics and OSMesa rendering on the CPU. Build: ~3 GB of free disk, network
  access to github.com and pypi.org, a few minutes. Run: ~1 GB of RAM per simulator process.

Build (from the repository root)
  docker build -f images/autobio/Dockerfile -t ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/eai-autobio:$(cat images/autobio/VERSION) .

Check a build
  Every load of a task checks the hashes of the compiled model and of the initial state in its instance.json and
  refuses to grade if either differs, so a build whose physics differs shows up as grader_error, never as a silent
  change. To check one task: harbor run -p tasks/autobio-pickup-i00-privileged -a oracle
  That the experts are gone: docker run --rm <image> python - <<< "import os; os.chdir('/opt/AutoBio/autobio');
  import pickup_centrifuge_tube as m; print(hasattr(m.Pickup, 'Expert'))" prints False.
